#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The mutants: one data list, read by run.sh (README.md says when and how).

Each mutant: an id, a file, `old` — text that must occur EXACTLY ONCE in that file — the
`new` text it becomes, the suites that should notice, and what is expected: "killed",
or "survives: not held — <what slips through>" for a gap the suites are known to leave.
An equivalent mutant is not kept: it proves nothing on any run. Anchored by text,
never by line number: a hook edit that moves an anchor updates the mutant in the same
change, and one whose `old` no longer occurs once is an error, not a skip.

    python3 -I mutation/mutants.py list [id ...]   # id, file, suite paths, expect (TSV)
    python3 -I mutation/mutants.py apply <id> <tree>
    python3 -I mutation/mutants.py check <tree>     # every anchor occurs exactly once
    python3 -I mutation/mutants.py verify <sha> <stamp-file>   # the release check's test

The stamp is the git note run.sh writes on the commit after a clean run of the whole
kit (refs/notes/mutation): its summary line, then one row per mutant — id, result, the
suite that killed it. `verify` holds it to this list as the commit has it: every mutant
once, none extra, every mutant expected killed was killed, and the line clean and of <sha>.
So a typed summary line, a selection, a stale kit or another commit's run fails. A known
gap now killed passes, named in the line it prints: the kit got stronger, and its expect
is to update (owner, 2026-11-05). One killed only by an abort or a timeout passes too,
named apart: that is no proof a suite noticed it, so it is run alone before its expect
changes.
"""
